  "facets": [
    {
      "title": "How it works",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "In the tagged MongoDB allocator implementation, `getMongoMaxCpuCacheSize` checks `MONGO_TCMALLOC_PER_CPU_CACHE_SIZE_BYTES` first and returns its parsed value when nonzero, before calculating the memory-derived default. The override therefore bypasses that default calculation. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mongodb/mongo/r8.0.16/src/third_party/tcmalloc/dist/tcmalloc/cpu_cache.cc",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:999322f8cc5a73ed2991ff8fb2437fe9293efcf1421975cdd8dd6872b5362c00",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:4340cbef1da210598cf6c5404c0d4cfb4b9da21fcde1de53703d3e8730a3d8c9",
            "ev:5eaff8162ebb6ce707df130735fac963d77ef6c6125bc0778d41ffd22bb94a08"
          ],
          "temporal": {
            "applicability_version": "8.0.16"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Problems, failure modes and limitations",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "In the tagged parameter handlers, setting `tcmallocMaxPerCPUCacheSize` without the Google allocator build, or `tcmallocMaxTotalThreadCacheBytes` without the legacy gperftools allocator build, logs an unavailable/no-effect warning and returns `Status::OK()`. An accepted setting alone therefore does not establish that its cache control took effect. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mongodb/mongo/r8.0.16/src/mongo/util/tcmalloc_set_parameter.cpp",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:290e068230c9fedebe866695024af6785e96f16da94514a75f1376a66307ccca",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:0441ddc95c397784b98befe8baa0fb0b75b7cbb6d8e73686983d5ecf50251421"
          ],
          "temporal": {
            "applicability_version": "8.0.16"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Upstream TCMalloc says memory release can reduce footprint after a peak but does not reduce the peak itself. It advises provisioning jobs for peak memory usage to avoid OOM errors and says release-rate tuning cannot substitute for appropriate job memory requirements. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://github.com/google/tcmalloc/blob/master/docs/tuning.md",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:5b090fe44a2defd75d222a65715a371e863b9be02358849d544c4e2a546b7bcc",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:1d3eb79de14298c2f4eb487201dfe9e62bdc6c6303098fb2196cf6626b3f34c6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "A static comparison of the MongoDB source tags for releases 8.0.16 and 8.0.17 finds their inspected allocator files byte-identical, including `parseLineFromFile`, `getSystemMemorySizeBytes`, and `getMemorySizeLimitInBytes`. This finite pair does not demonstrate a release-to-release parser change or establish other tags or compiled builds. Separately, the pinned Linux cgroup v2 documentation defines `memory.max` as a memory-usage hard limit with default `max`; its limits convention says `max` is a no-op. This is source comparison and interface documentation, not an executed container test. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mongodb/mongo/r8.0.16/src/third_party/tcmalloc/dist/tcmalloc/cpu_cache.cc",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:3c37250253126e9864bb642fc9dbb6e37eb343403c1c9ad22afee6edf5ad2624",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:048e5ef3c2c73ee0c4415156b1c8745cc0c35e3add7c911354763425c6dde163",
            "ev:4f08faabff156edf5f4f4a78d28270a63af6bf7d9eefa6150373c8c538e3097b",
